1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,777
A spool of thread.

2
00:00:01,777 --> 00:00:03,046
A packed suitcase.

3
00:00:03,046 --> 00:00:04,268
A school library.

4
00:00:04,668 --> 00:00:08,821
In biology class, the teacher said
one human cell holds about two metres

5
00:00:08,821 --> 00:00:09,721
of DNA.

6
00:00:09,931 --> 00:00:14,136
Diya looked at her fingertip, made of
millions of cells too small to see.

7
00:00:14,136 --> 00:00:18,264
She wants to be a doctor, and she
asked: where does two metres of thread

8
00:00:18,264 --> 00:00:19,164
fit?

9
00:00:19,057 --> 00:00:22,671
The answer is the nucleus,
the control room of the cell.

10
00:00:22,671 --> 00:00:25,535
There, the thread is
packed into chromosomes.

11
00:00:26,035 --> 00:00:30,547
By the end, you will see inside the
nucleus, watch chromatin pack into

12
00:00:30,547 --> 00:00:33,654
chromosomes, and name
four kinds of chromosome.

13
00:00:34,304 --> 00:00:35,801
First, a little history.

14
00:00:36,151 --> 00:00:40,036
The nucleus was first described
by Robert Brown, as early as 1831.

15
00:00:40,486 --> 00:00:44,768
Later, Flemming saw a material inside
it that took up basic dyes and turned

16
00:00:44,768 --> 00:00:45,668
coloured.

17
00:00:45,455 --> 00:00:46,892
He named it chromatin.

18
00:00:47,342 --> 00:00:50,734
Chromatin is made of DNA, some basic
proteins called histones, some non-histone

19
00:00:50,734 --> 00:00:51,731
proteins, and RNA.

20
00:00:52,381 --> 00:00:55,643
Let's zoom into a cell,
one level at a time.

21
00:00:55,993 --> 00:00:57,909
Most cells have one nucleus.

22
00:00:57,909 --> 00:01:02,518
In a cell that is not dividing, it
holds long fine threads of chromatin,

23
00:01:02,518 --> 00:01:06,872
a jelly called the nuclear matrix,
and one or more round bodies called

24
00:01:06,872 --> 00:01:07,772
nucleoli.

25
00:01:08,119 --> 00:01:10,619
A nucleolus has no membrane around it.

26
00:01:10,619 --> 00:01:12,924
It is where ribosomal RNA is made.

27
00:01:12,924 --> 00:01:16,931
Cells that make a lot of protein
have bigger and more nucleoli.

28
00:01:17,431 --> 00:01:18,638
Zoom into the edge.

29
00:01:18,638 --> 00:01:22,477
The nuclear envelope is two membranes,
with a gap between them called the

30
00:01:22,477 --> 00:01:25,134
perinuclear space, ten
to fifty nanometres wide.

31
00:01:25,134 --> 00:01:28,487
The outer membrane often joins
the ER, and carries ribosomes.

32
00:01:28,987 --> 00:01:30,023
Zoom into a pore.

33
00:01:30,023 --> 00:01:33,479
In many places, the two membranes
join and leave tiny openings, called

34
00:01:33,479 --> 00:01:34,379
nuclear pores.

35
00:01:34,307 --> 00:01:37,516
RNA and protein molecules pass
through them, in both directions.

36
00:01:38,166 --> 00:01:41,657
Now watch the thread get
packed, one step at a time.

37
00:01:42,007 --> 00:01:42,907
Step one.

38
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:46,950
In a resting cell, chromatin is
spread out, as long fine threads.

39
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:48,600
Step two.

40
00:01:48,398 --> 00:01:51,990
When the cell gets ready to
divide, each thread starts to coil.

41
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:53,640
Step three.

42
00:01:53,495 --> 00:01:55,267
The coils fold again and again.

43
00:01:55,267 --> 00:01:57,202
The thread becomes short and thick.

44
00:01:57,952 --> 00:01:58,852
Step four.

45
00:01:58,839 --> 00:02:00,776
Now we can see chromosomes.

46
00:02:00,776 --> 00:02:03,854
Chromosomes are visible
only in dividing cells.

47
00:02:04,604 --> 00:02:05,504
Step five.

48
00:02:05,433 --> 00:02:09,351
A human cell has forty six
chromosomes, in twenty three pairs.

49
00:02:09,351 --> 00:02:11,992
Together they share that two metre thread.

50
00:02:12,742 --> 00:02:15,000
Now look at one chromosome closely.

51
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:19,888
Every chromosome has a primary
constriction, a narrow waist, called the

52
00:02:19,888 --> 00:02:20,788
centromere.

53
00:02:21,263 --> 00:02:25,258
On the sides of the centromere sit
disc-shaped structures, called kinetochores.

54
00:02:25,758 --> 00:02:28,318
The centromere's position
gives four types.

55
00:02:28,318 --> 00:02:31,170
In the middle: metacentric,
two equal arms.

56
00:02:31,170 --> 00:02:35,439
A little off the middle: sub-metacentric,
one shorter arm and one longer

57
00:02:35,439 --> 00:02:36,339
arm.

58
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:41,228
Close to one end: acrocentric, one
extremely short arm and one very long

59
00:02:41,228 --> 00:02:42,128
arm.

60
00:02:41,758 --> 00:02:43,990
Right at the end: telocentric.

61
00:02:44,490 --> 00:02:48,774
A few chromosomes have a second,
non-staining constriction at a fixed place.

62
00:02:48,774 --> 00:02:52,151
It makes a small piece look
separate, called a satellite.

63
00:02:52,801 --> 00:02:53,858
Side by side.

64
00:02:53,858 --> 00:02:54,758
When.

65
00:02:54,406 --> 00:02:57,635
Chromatin is seen in a
cell that is not dividing.

66
00:02:57,635 --> 00:03:00,552
Chromosomes are seen
only in a dividing cell.

67
00:03:01,302 --> 00:03:02,202
The look.

68
00:03:02,065 --> 00:03:04,599
Chromatin is long, fine and spread out.

69
00:03:04,599 --> 00:03:07,467
Chromosomes are short,
thick and easy to see.

70
00:03:08,217 --> 00:03:09,117
The stuff.

71
00:03:08,879 --> 00:03:11,263
Both are the same material,
DNA with proteins.

72
00:03:11,263 --> 00:03:12,824
Only the packing is different.

73
00:03:13,574 --> 00:03:14,474
Your turn.

74
00:03:14,461 --> 00:03:18,664
A chromosome has one extremely
short arm and one very long arm.

75
00:03:18,664 --> 00:03:20,031
Which type is it?

76
00:03:20,031 --> 00:03:21,336
Pause and decide.

77
00:03:24,536 --> 00:03:25,436
Acrocentric!

78
00:03:25,404 --> 00:03:27,809
Its centromere sits close to one end.

79
00:03:28,459 --> 00:03:29,399
Remember this.

80
00:03:29,399 --> 00:03:32,555
Where the centromere sits
gives the chromosome its name.

81
00:03:33,155 --> 00:03:34,747
Now, a worked example.

82
00:03:34,747 --> 00:03:35,895
Four chromosomes.

83
00:03:35,895 --> 00:03:38,899
Where is the centromere,
and what is the name?

84
00:03:39,249 --> 00:03:40,433
Two equal arms.

85
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:42,651
The centromere is in the middle.

86
00:03:42,651 --> 00:03:43,564
Metacentric.

87
00:03:44,014 --> 00:03:46,318
One shorter arm, one longer arm.

88
00:03:46,318 --> 00:03:47,545
Near the middle.

89
00:03:47,545 --> 00:03:48,602
Sub-metacentric.

90
00:03:49,052 --> 00:03:52,273
One extremely short
arm, one very long arm.

91
00:03:52,273 --> 00:03:53,440
Near the end.

92
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:54,423
Acrocentric.

93
00:03:54,873 --> 00:03:56,602
No second arm at all.

94
00:03:56,602 --> 00:03:59,164
The centromere is at the very end.

95
00:03:59,164 --> 00:04:00,147
Telocentric.

96
00:04:00,797 --> 00:04:02,261
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:02,611 --> 00:04:05,581
Many students think every
living cell has a nucleus.

98
00:04:05,981 --> 00:04:08,070
But some mature cells lack one.

99
00:04:08,070 --> 00:04:12,556
The red blood cells of many mammals,
and the sieve tube cells of vascular

100
00:04:12,556 --> 00:04:14,287
plants, have no nucleus.

101
00:04:14,937 --> 00:04:16,319
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:16,319 --> 00:04:20,474
In board papers, as a labelled diagram
of the nucleus or the chromosome

103
00:04:20,474 --> 00:04:23,275
types, and in NEET, as
statement questions.

104
00:04:23,875 --> 00:04:25,022
Exam level now.

105
00:04:25,022 --> 00:04:26,811
Which statement is correct?

106
00:04:26,811 --> 00:04:28,006
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:33,406 --> 00:04:34,815
The answer is B.

108
00:04:35,315 --> 00:04:39,312
Nuclear pores let RNA and proteins
pass in both directions, not only out.

109
00:04:39,312 --> 00:04:41,928
And chromosomes are seen
only in dividing cells.

110
00:04:42,428 --> 00:04:46,909
A telocentric chromosome has its
centromere at the end, not the middle.

111
00:04:46,909 --> 00:04:48,257
Only B is correct.

112
00:04:48,857 --> 00:04:50,397
Here is your recap.

113
00:04:50,397 --> 00:04:51,980
Screenshot this card.

114
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:57,366
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:04:57,766 --> 00:04:58,666
One.

116
00:04:58,089 --> 00:04:59,390
What is the perinuclear space?

117
00:05:00,290 --> 00:05:01,190
Two.

118
00:05:00,754 --> 00:05:04,064
Name the four types of chromosome
by centromere position.

119
00:05:04,964 --> 00:05:05,864
Three.

120
00:05:05,487 --> 00:05:08,712
Name two kinds of cells that
have no nucleus when mature.

121
00:05:09,812 --> 00:05:14,472
Next time: if you burn a living thing,
what is left, and what does it tell

122
00:05:14,472 --> 00:05:15,372
us?

123
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:18,006
We find out in how to
analyse chemical composition.
